\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The crocodile metaphor immediately arrests attention. Pharaoh is 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1514 \u1463 \u1468 \u1504 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1490 \u1464 \u1468 \u1491 \u1493 \u1465 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1464 \u1512 \u1465 \u1489 \u1461 \u1509 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1498 \u1456 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  \'97 "the great dragon/crocodile crouching in the midst of his Nile channels." The verb 
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1465 \u1489 \u1461 \u1509 
\f1  suggests not just resting but lying in wait, the posture of a predator who owns his territory so completely he need not hunt actively.\
But the claim that draws YHWH's opposition is precise: 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1469 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  \'97 "My Nile is mine, and I made myself."\
<notes>This differs crucially from Tyre's claim. The king of Tyre said 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  \'97 "I am god" \'97 claiming divine status through wisdom and commercial success. Pharaoh claims something more fundamental: self-origination. Not "I am god" but "I made myself." The Nile's predictable flooding, unlike rain-dependent agriculture, created the illusion of a self-sustaining system independent of any external source.</notes>\
The hooks in the jaws (
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1495 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1500 \u1456 \u1495 \u1464 \u1497 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 ) echo chapter 19's treatment of the lion cubs \'97 the same vocabulary for capturing dangerous predators. But here there's an additional element: the fish of the Nile stick to Pharaoh's scales (
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1492 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1511 \u1456 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1456 \u1490 \u1463 \u1514 \u1470 \u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1511 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1511 \u1456 \u1513 \u1465 \u1474 \u1514 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1 ). When the crocodile is dragged from his channels, the entire ecosystem that depended on him comes too.\
For exiles who had looked to Egypt as alternative to Babylon \'97 the reed staff metaphor in verses 6-7 makes this explicit \'97 the message is devastating. Egypt wasn't just unreliable; Egypt's fundamental self-understanding was false. The Nile wasn't self-created. The system wasn't self-sustaining. The power Israel leaned on had no foundation except the claim to have made itself.\
What strikes me most: the accusation isn't idolatry in the conventional sense. Pharaoh doesn't claim to worship false gods. He claims to be the source of his own existence and prosperity. This is a different species of hubris than Tyre's wisdom-corruption or the 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1462 \u1488 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1  of chapter 25's neighbors.
