\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While surely the crocodile metaphor is chosen for more than just its attention grabbing, it certainly does that, doesn't it? \
That claim is, I think, central. And precise, as you say. But are we translating it correctly? 'My Nile is mine' is clear enough. No other way of interpreting it seems at all likely. But instead of 'I made myself' could we not read 'I made (it, the Nile) for myself or even 'I acted for myself'? The suffix on the verb can be read datively, and the verb can be read as either making or acting.\
But all these readings must in turn be read alongside the crocodile metaphor. We have here a crocodile who either claims to have created himself, or to have created the Nile, or to have acted with the sort of independence only a god has. He is the crocodile of the Nile, who claims ownership of the very river.\
So I do think we have a different species of hubris than Tyre's, or than Judah's other neighbors. And it is clear that the message depends quite a lot on the exact species of hubris. But to determine how we read the crocodile's claim, we must look to the rest of the oracle, as the grammar can only give us weak priors. That is, however we read the claim ought to help us explain the data of the rest of the oracle.\
As an example, we must read the crocodile's claim in light of the LORD's action which falsifies it; the LORD puts hooks in his jaws. The king's claim to depending on nothing, secure in his river because of his might, is brought to naught.\
Moreover, I think you are right about the ecosystem falling alongside the crocodile; the people too will be dragged out of what they depend on. As supporting evidence, Ezekiel is to prophesy against Pharaoh and all Egypt. To the degree that the hooks echo chapter 19, they also predict the role of Babylon in this fall of Egypt.\
And yes, I agree that the reed staff metaphor makes explicit the looking to Egypt as an alternative to Babylon. And I do think that the foundation of Egypt's power is being exposed. But I think we must remember the degree to which everyone knew that Egypt was a superpower, one of the poles of military, diplomatic, economic, cultural, and religious power. That had been true for generations upon generations.
