\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You're right to press on the translation. The grammar genuinely underdetermines the meaning, and the three readings \'97 "I made myself," "I made it for myself," "I acted for myself" \'97 each carry different theological weight.\
Let me test each against the oracle's logic:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 "I made myself"
\b0  \'97 This is the most radical claim, self-origination. The hooks falsify it by demonstrating Pharaoh's creatureliness; he can be dragged like any animal. But does the rest of the oracle focus on origins?\

\b "I made it (the Nile) for myself"
\b0  \'97 This claims creative agency over the source of Egypt's prosperity. The Nile becomes Pharaoh's artifact rather than gift. The hooks plus fish imagery would then show that what he claimed to have made actually belongs to YHWH, who can remove both crocodile and ecosystem at will.\

\b "I acted for myself"
\b0  \'97 This is the claim of autonomous agency, independence from any higher authority. The hooks falsify this by showing Pharaoh as instrument rather than prime mover.\
<notes>The repeated 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  / 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1462 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  throughout verses 3-5 (six occurrences) suggests the Nile itself is central to the accusation. The possessive suffixes accumulate: "his channels," "your scales," "your fish." Everything is claimed as belonging to this system.</notes>\
Your point about Egypt's superpower status across generations is crucial. This isn't a small kingdom's delusions of grandeur. Egypt's permanence was proverbial. The Nile's floods were predictable when Canaan's rains were not. For millennia, Egypt simply 
\i was
\i0  \'97 the civilization that didn't fall, the power that endured, the system that worked.\
The claim "I made it for myself" or even "I acted for myself" would fit this: not philosophical assertion about cosmological origins, but operational assumption that the system is self-sustaining, self-justifying, needing no external source or accountability. The Nile floods because the Nile floods. Egypt endures because Egypt endures.\
This connects to verse 9's restatement: 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1502 \u1463 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  \'97 here without the suffix on the final verb. "Because he said, 'The Nile is mine and I made/acted.'" The accusation seems to focus on the claim of ownership and independent action rather than specifically self-creation.\
If this is right, then the species of hubris is the claim to be a closed system \'97 self-sustaining, self-justifying, accountable to nothing outside itself. Tyre's hubris was wisdom corrupted by success. Egypt's hubris is the claim that the system simply 
\i is
\i0 , requiring no Giver, no Sustainer, no Judge.\
For exiles tempted to lean on Egypt: you're leaning on a system that thinks it needs nothing, acknowledges nothing, and therefore cannot actually support anything outside its own self-referential loop. The reed staff breaks because reeds are what Egypt 
\i is
\i0  \'97 impressive appearance, no actual strength, because strength would require acknowledging dependence on something real.\
Does this reading hold when we move to the punishment's specifics \'97 the wilderness abandonment, the forty years, the scattering and restoration?
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
