\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes, verse 9 is a crucial piece of evidence as to how we should read the crocodile's claim. Slightly different words, but the same claim - and the ambiguous parts are identical. But the therefore that goes with this because can help us understand how we should understand the claim, and not just grammatically. The LORD's opposition is repeated from verse 3, and implied by verse 2. While that is by no means unimportant or to be taken lightly, it does not well resolve how we read the claim, at least not on its own. \
While the rest of the oracle focusing on origins as such would be good evidence for "I made myself", I am not sure its lack ought to be taken as dispostitive. While I raised alternatives to that interpretation, you are of course welcome to argue for it if other evidence can be found.\
On the other hand, as you note, the other theories have the advantage that they explain what the hooks have to do with the accusation.\
Similarly, I do think that your theory, that the hubris is a claim to be a closed system, fits much of the evidence in these verses. And such a claim would need to be corrected for Egypt to know the LORD is the LORD. \
But the exact nature of this theory is, I think in doubt. A closed system is one possibility. But so is power projection, or sovereignty, and there may be others. But I agree that the closed system fits the evidence we have so far examined.\
So our next task is to examine the rest of the evidence.\
The wilderness abandonment, forty years, and scattering somewhat match the drawing out of the Nile with hooks, with the fish caught along stuck to the scales of the crocodile.\
As for the punishment, it's match to the wilderness forty years of Israel before entering the promised land - and that after leaving Egypt - means that at the very least, what befalls Egypt will be some sort of enacted sign. \
Moreover, it seems not at all a coincidence that scattering is threatened, as it was against Israel for breaking covenant as in Deuteronomy 28:64.\
But while breaking the power of Egypt in this way fits all of the theories of what the crocodile claims, while fitting acting for myself somewhat better, resolving the degree to which that is true would require working through how the reed metaphor informs the punishment.\
And that, in turn, perhaps ought to wait until we have put what we have already mentioned on firmer footing. Your brief summary of the reed metaphor is accurate, and will do until we can look into that matter in more depth.
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
