\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let me try to put what we have on firmer footing by examining the movement from accusation to punishment more carefully.\
The sequence in verses 3-5 is precise: hooks in jaws 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  fish stick to scales 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  dragged up from channels 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  abandoned to wilderness 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  left unburied for beasts and birds. The crocodile who claimed ownership of the Nile is removed from it entirely, and everything that depended on him (the fish, his ecosystem) comes out with him but to destruction.\
The verb 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1504 \u1456 \u1496 \u1463 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1498 \u1464 
\f1  in verse 5 \'97 "I will abandon you" \'97 carries weight. This isn't just killing but casting away, leaving in the wilderness (
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1502 \u1460 \u1468 \u1491 \u1456 \u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1512 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 ). The crocodile of the Nile ends up in the place that is the Nile's opposite: waterless, lifeless, the negation of everything Egypt represented.\
<notes>The denial of burial \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1461 \u1488 \u1464 \u1505 \u1461 \u1507 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1511 \u1464 \u1468 \u1489 \u1461 \u1509 
\f1  \'97 echoes covenant curses. Deuteronomy 28:26 threatens that corpses will be food for birds and beasts with none to frighten them away. This formula appears throughout the prophets as mark of ultimate disgrace. But here it's applied to Egypt, not Israel.</notes>\
The recognition formula in verse 6 is striking: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1497 \u1464 \u1491 \u1456 \u1506 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1464 \u1500 \u1470 \u1497 \u1465 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1489 \u1461 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1510 \u1456 \u1512 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1492 \u1465 \u1493 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1  \'97 "all inhabitants of Egypt will know that I am YHWH." Not Pharaoh specifically, but all who dwell in Egypt. The crocodile's fate teaches the entire population.\
And the 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  that follows gives the reason, but it shifts: 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1457 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1514 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1506 \u1462 \u1504 \u1462 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1511 \u1464 \u1504 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1489 \u1461 \u1497 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1474 \u1512 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1500 
\f1  \'97 "because they were a reed staff to the house of Israel."\
This is where I need to think more carefully. The accusation against Pharaoh (verses 3, 9) is about his claim regarding the Nile and his own making/acting. But the reason given for Egypt's judgment in verse 6 is their function as unreliable support for Israel. These seem to be two different accusations \'97 or are they connected?\
The reed staff that splinters when grasped, piercing the hand and making loins unsteady (verse 7) \'97 this describes what happens when Israel leans on Egypt. But why does Egypt's self-sufficiency claim connect to Egypt's failure as support?\
Perhaps: a system that claims to need nothing outside itself cannot actually give anything outside itself. Egypt's "I made it for myself" or "I acted for myself" means Egypt exists for Egypt. When Israel sought support from such a system, they sought something that by its own self-understanding has nothing to offer. The reed breaks not despite Egypt's power but because of what Egypt's power is \'97 self-referential, self-serving, incapable of genuine support because genuine support would require acknowledging relationship, dependence, gift.\
The closed system cannot be leaned on precisely because it admits no outside.\
Does this connection hold, or am I forcing coherence where the text maintains two separate accusations?
