\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Whether there is a connection between the accusations is a key question for interpretation.\
However, in answering that question, we must remember that while the grammatical audience is Egypt and its king, the exiles of Judah are part of the audience as well. Consider the date notice; Jerusalem is under siege by Babylon, and the exiles may yet be hoping that the power of Egypt might yet rescue the city. This hope had everything to do with Egypt's actual actions and policy. Jeremiah 37 records Egyptian army maneuvers along more or less exactly those lines, though obviously not ultimately successful. And Ezekiel 17 records the LORD's rejection of that hope on the grounds that the LORD decides what happens, and not even the might of Egypt would be of any help because Babylon was sent by the LORD to punish. Moreover, in Ezekiel 17, the LORD says that part of what is being punished is turning to Egypt for help.\
Thus, Egypt is acting as an alternative to keeping covenant with the LORD and with Babylon. A false, unreliable alternative. Egypt may think it can influence events using armies - but it will find that it cannot even protect itself, much less Jerusalem. For its own sake, it must know this. For their own sake, the Jewish exiles living in Babylon must know this. For the sake of the LORD's name, all nations must know this.\
What is demonstrated goes far beyond attacking an idea of a closed system, if it attacks that at all. Any sort of dominance over other nations - and for a time, even over themselves - is denied Egypt. And this, I think, has everything to do with the belief that because they have a large army (and for thousands of years, had been able to project power in ways others feared), they are the ones who decide what happens with the nations, not the LORD. The LORD demonstrated once before that he could take a nation of slaves, even from Egyptian territory itself, even on turf claimed by Egypt's gods and protected by Egypt's army. So now, he makes the claim before it happens that he will break that power projection. And so it came to pass.\
While this happened after the dating of Ezekiel 29, Jeremiah 44 continues to warn against putting trust in Egypt, in this case by fleeing there as exiles. Jeremiah 44 explicitly invokes the coming devastation of Egypt as a reason not to flee there for protection, and uses a similar broken reed metaphor. Whether trusting for military aid or trusting that military to make a safe haven to flee to, trusting Egypt is something that the LORD warns against.\
In the same way Jeremiah 44 parallels the broken reed metaphor, Jeremiah 46 parallels the Nile metaphor. There, Egypt thinks it will rise like the Nile, and thus cover the earth, destroying cities and people. Jeremiah 46 explicitly connects this Nile language, power projection against other nations, trusting in Egypt, and God's people being restored but by the power and protection of the LORD. This is not proof that Ezekiel saw such a connection, but it provides a possible such connection.\
Isaiah, unlike these chapters of Jeremiah, was clearly known to Ezekiel. Isaiah 30 and 31 are written in the context of Assyrian invasion, not Babylonian, but Egypt had been a potential source of false hope for a long time; that is what makes the prediction that its power projection will be broken so striking. Isaiah 30 and 31 also warn that Egypt's help is empty, diagnosing the problem as seeing the world in terms of who has the strongest army, forgetting what God might do. "The Egyptians are man, and not God, and their horses are flesh, and not spirit. When the Lord stretches out his hand,\'a0the helper will stumble, and he who is helped will fall, and they will all perish together."
\f0 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Opus:\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This reframes the accusation significantly. The crocodile's claim isn't primarily metaphysical ("I am a closed system") but operational ("I decide what happens").\

\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1488 \u1458 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1504 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  \'97 reading this in light of your evidence, the claim becomes: "The Nile is mine and I act" \'97 that is, I am the one who acts, I am the agent who determines outcomes. The Nile represents not just prosperity but the basis for power projection. Egypt's armies march because Egypt's agriculture sustains them. Egypt intervenes in Canaan, in Syria, wherever Egypt chooses, because the Nile makes Egypt capable of projecting force.\
<notes>The crocodile crouching in his channels isn't just secure; he's poised. 
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1465 \u1489 \u1461 \u1509 
\f1  is the predator's posture before action. This is a power that sees itself as the prime mover in regional affairs \'97 not merely self-sustaining but self-directing, choosing when and where to act.</notes>\
The Isaiah 31 quotation is decisive for understanding the category of error: "The Egyptians are man, and not God, and their horses are flesh, and not spirit." The problem isn't that Egypt claims to be divine in some theological sense. The problem is that Egypt (and those who trust Egypt) treat Egyptian power as the relevant variable in determining outcomes. They calculate: who has the stronger army? They forget: YHWH stretches out his hand.\
This explains why the two accusations connect. Pharaoh's claim ("I act") and Egypt's failure as support for Israel are the same error seen from two angles. Pharaoh thinks he decides what happens. Israel thinks Egypt's decision-making power is real and worth trusting. Both are wrong because neither accounts for YHWH.\
The hooks in the jaws then become precise refutation. The one who claims to act, to be the agent determining outcomes, is himself acted upon. He doesn't choose to leave the Nile; he is dragged out. The fish stuck to his scales \'97 Egypt's dependent peoples, client states, those who relied on Egyptian power \'97 come out with him but to destruction. The entire system that Egypt's "action" supposedly sustained collapses because that action was never the real cause.\
The wilderness abandonment gains force from this reading. Egypt ends up in the anti-Nile not merely as punishment but as demonstration. The power that claimed to act from the security of abundant waters finds itself in the place where no action avails, where there is nothing to project, nothing to sustain projection. The 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1491 \u1456 \u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1  is where Egyptian categories of power simply do not apply.\
And the forty years \'97 this must be deliberate echo. Israel wandered forty years after leaving Egypt, learning dependence on YHWH rather than Egyptian resources. Now Egypt itself will experience a kind of wilderness wandering, stripped of everything that made "I act" seem plausible.\
