\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your personal answer cuts to the heart of it. The temptation isn't to worship Egypt's gods or adopt Egypt's theology. It's to calculate as Egypt calculates \'97 to see armies, resources, strategic position as the relevant variables. To treat "what can this nation do?" as the operative question rather than "what has YHWH spoken?"\
This is why Egypt's form of hubris is peculiarly dangerous to covenant people. Tyre's "I am god" is obviously idolatrous. You can reject it while still falling into Egypt's error. You can maintain orthodox theology \'97 YHWH is God, there is no other \'97 while operationally treating military strength as what determines outcomes. The confession and the calculation can coexist. Israel proved this repeatedly.\
<notes>The exodus narrative establishes Egypt as the paradigmatic site of this temptation. Israel left Egypt but kept calculating in Egyptian terms. The spies' report in Numbers 13-14 is exactly this: they saw fortified cities, giants, military impossibility. They calculated. They forgot what YHWH had done to Egypt itself. The forty years followed because they trusted their assessment of military reality over YHWH's word.</notes>\
And this is what makes the forty years for Egypt so pointed. Israel wandered forty years for treating Egyptian-style calculation as wisdom. Now Egypt itself will experience that wilderness \'97 stripped of everything that made such calculation seem reasonable. The anti-Nile, where there is no basis for "I act."\
Your Genesis 11 reference \'97 "nothing they propose to do will now be impossible for them" \'97 this is the same species. Babel's hubris wasn't claiming to be divine but claiming that human coordination and technology could secure outcomes. "Let us make a name for ourselves, lest we be scattered." The scattering of Egypt (verse 12: 
