\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1463 \u1468 \u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1 ) echoes this. What they feared, what their power projection was meant to prevent, comes upon them precisely through that power's failure.\
The fear of the LORD as beginning of wisdom \'97 this reframes the entire oracle. Egypt's failure isn't lack of wisdom in the worldly sense. Egyptian civilization was genuinely sophisticated, their engineering remarkable, their administration effective. But wisdom that begins anywhere other than the fear of YHWH isn't wisdom at all. It's sophisticated foolishness. It calculates correctly within a frame that excludes the only variable that matters.\
For the exiles hearing this: every time you're tempted to ask "but what about Babylon's army? What about Egypt's army? What about the realistic political situation?" \'97 you're doing what Egypt does. You're saying "I act" or "they act" when the question is "what has YHWH spoken?"\
The recognition formula then becomes not just information but reorientation. To know that YHWH is YHWH is to stop calculating in Egyptian terms. It's to fear YHWH rather than nations. It's to treat YHWH's word as the relevant variable rather than military assessments.\
