\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The shift is notable. Earlier oracles addressed the exiles' immediate situation \'97 their false hopes about Jerusalem, their understanding of why judgment came, their temptation to blame ancestors or claim victim status. This oracle, while still relevant to exiles hoping in Egyptian intervention, points more explicitly beyond.\
The exiles of 571 BCE hearing year 27's addition would already know Jerusalem had fallen. The immediate question of Egyptian military aid was settled. Yet YHWH speaks again about Egypt, adds the Babylon-as-laborer passage, and concludes with horn and opened mouth. Why speak this to people for whom Egypt's failure to save Jerusalem is already history?\
Perhaps because the deeper lesson wasn't yet learned. They knew Egypt hadn't saved them. But did they know 
\i why
\i0  \'97 that Egypt's fundamental self-understanding was false, that "I act" was always a lie, that trusting any power that claims independent agency is trusting a reed? The oracle teaches not just "Egypt failed" but "Egypt was always going to fail because Egypt's claim was never true."\
<notes>The pattern of superpowers claiming agency persists beyond any single historical moment. Babylon would fall to Persia. Persia to Greece. Greece to Rome. Each claiming to act, to determine outcomes. The oracle against Egypt becomes paradigmatic \'97 not just about Egypt but about every power that says "my river is mine and I made it" in whatever form.</notes>\
This is where future readers \'97 including us \'97 find ourselves addressed. The specific powers change; the temptation remains constant. Calculate in terms of who has the strongest army, the most resources, the best strategic position. Forget that YHWH stretches out his hand and helper and helped fall together.\
Your earlier personal reflection applies here. The United States isn't Egypt, but the temptation to trust in its apparent stability, to calculate outcomes based on its power projection, to treat its agency as the relevant variable \'97 this is Egypt's error in contemporary form. The oracle doesn't just inform about ancient geopolitics. It diagnoses a persistent human tendency and declares YHWH's consistent response.\
The horn and opened mouth then become promises that extend as far as the temptation extends. Wherever and whenever people are tempted to trust powers that claim "I act," there the word about YHWH's actual agency needs speaking. And there the Davidic hope \'97 power exercised in dependence on YHWH rather than in claimed independence \'97 offers the alternative.\
What remains for us to examine in this chapter? We've traced the accusation, the punishment, the connection to Israel's temptation, the Babylon addition, and the closing promises. Are there elements we've neglected?
