\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 There is what Ezekiel said would happen to Egypt, and what we know of what in fact did happen.\
Ezekiel promised Babylon the land of Egypt. But the details are all about looting. Babylon's pay would be the wealth, spoil, and plunder. In practice, that means armies pouring inside the cities where that wealth was.\
This fits with the prediction of Jeremiah, that Nebuchadnezzar would make it as far as the palace. Moreover, it fits with extrabiblical sources claiming Nebuchadnezar made it as far as Libya - unlikely without going through Egypt. And it fits with what we know of chaos and downfall in Egypt itself at that time.\
But the 40 years does not fit cleanly. To be sure, there was a generation of chaos and weakness, and then a weakened kingdom that not only never became a superpower again, but was often under the thumb of one superpower or another. But the apparent Torah reference seems to be doing something other than giving a timeframe for a particular event.\
And yes, anywhere stubborn children make plans, but not the LORD's plans, and alliance, but not of the Lord's Spirit, there is a need for this warning, this reevaluation, this alternative.\'a0 Isaiah said as much.\'a0 Ezekiel as well, but with much different rhetoric.
