\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The forty years as Torah echo rather than precise timeframe \'97 this seems right. The number signals category more than chronology. Israel's forty years wasn't about duration but about what happened: a generation that trusted human calculation over YHWH's word died out, and a new generation entered the land. Egypt's forty years would then mean: the Egypt that said "I act" must pass away, and what emerges will be something humbled, incapable of that claim.\
The historical reality \'97 chaos, weakness, subjugation to successive empires \'97 fulfills the substance without matching a precise calendar. Egypt never again projected power as it had. Persia ruled it, then Greece, then Rome. The nation that claimed to act became a nation acted upon. The crocodile dragged from his channels and left in the wilderness is metaphor for a geopolitical reality that persisted for millennia.\
<notes>The specificity of Babylon's payment \'97 
