\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 One never exhausts the details of a particular verse or section of Scripture.\'a0 But what I think we need to do before concluding is to understand how that reed staff metaphor fits into the overall sweep of Ezekiel's rhetoric to the exiles in that oracle.\
Ezekiel has spoken in many previous chapters of the LORD who both speaks and performs his word.\
Still, that was clearly not the worldview of his audience. So Ezekiel must do more than assert these things. What is his rhetorical strategy, speaking into a world which does not naturally see the LORD as sovereign, but instead fears and trusts agricultural production, armies, and diplomacy?\'a0 How does the reed staff metaphor fit into a unified rhetorical strategy?
