\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The rhetorical strategy moves from exposure to demonstration to reorientation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Exposure:
\b0  The crocodile metaphor takes Egypt's self-understanding and makes it visible as claim. Pharaoh didn't announce "I am the source of my own power" in those words. He simply operated that way \'97 secure in his channels, projecting force, treating the Nile system as his own. Ezekiel's "my Nile is mine and I act" isn't quotation but diagnosis. He names what Egypt assumed, making the implicit explicit so it can be judged.\

\b Demonstration:
\b0  The hooks, the dragging out, the wilderness abandonment \'97 these don't just punish but prove. The one who claimed to act is acted upon. The one who claimed ownership is dispossessed. The demonstration isn't merely that YHWH is stronger (which could leave Egyptian categories intact, just with YHWH as bigger crocodile). It's that Egypt's categories were wrong. "I act" was never true of Egypt because only YHWH acts in the sense that matters.\

\b Reorientation:
\b0  Here the reed staff enters. The exiles already knew Egypt was powerful. They'd calculated: Babylon threatens, Egypt might counterbalance, perhaps Egyptian alliance could save Jerusalem. The reed staff metaphor meets them in their calculation and reframes it.\
The reed looks like a staff. It appears to offer support. When you're drowning in political crisis, you reach for whatever seems solid. But the reed isn't solid \'97 not because it lacks material substance but because its substance is the wrong kind for what you need. It splinters under weight. Worse, it wounds the one who trusted it: 
