\f1 ). The one who leaned on Egypt isn't just unsupported but damaged. The trust itself caused harm. This isn't neutral failure but active wounding.</notes>\
The rhetorical movement then becomes: You thought you were calculating wisely when you sought Egyptian alliance. You saw a staff and reached for it. But you misidentified what you were grasping. Your categories \'97 who has the stronger army, who can project power \'97 led you to see support where there was only splinter-prone reed. And now your shoulder is torn.\
The unified strategy addresses an audience that doesn't naturally see YHWH as sovereign by showing them where their natural seeing led. You saw Egypt as power; Egypt saw itself as source. Both were wrong, and both wrongnesses connect. Egypt couldn't support you because Egypt's self-understanding was false. The Nile wasn't Egypt's; Egypt didn't act. And therefore Egypt couldn't give what it didn't have \'97 reliable agency, genuine support, actual power to determine outcomes.\
The recognition formula then completes the reorientation: 
